<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Jakarta-Persistence on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/jakarta-persistence/</link><description>Recent content in Jakarta-Persistence on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Sun, 29 Mar 2026 19:32:52 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/jakarta-persistence/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Jakarta Persistence, Hibernate, and Spring Data JPA</title><link>https://example.org/jakarta-persistence-hibernate-and-spring-data-jpa/</link><pubDate>Sun, 29 Mar 2026 19:32:52 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/jakarta-persistence-hibernate-and-spring-data-jpa/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Jakarta Persistence defines the standard Java API for object-relational
mapping. Hibernate is a widely used implementation. Spring Data JPA builds
repository abstractions on top of a Jakarta Persistence provider.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="the-jakarta-persistence-model"&gt;
 The Jakarta Persistence Model
 &lt;a href="#the-jakarta-persistence-model" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;An application maps ordinary classes to database tables with annotations such
as &lt;code&gt;@Entity&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;@Table&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;@Id&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;@Column&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="jose-wrapper"&gt;
 &lt;div class="jose-tools"&gt; &lt;span&gt;java&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;button&gt;Copy&lt;/button&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="jose-inner"&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-java" data-lang="java"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;@Entity&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;@Table&lt;/span&gt;(name &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;orders&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;PurchaseOrder&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;@Id&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;@GeneratedValue&lt;/span&gt;(strategy &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; GenerationType.&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;IDENTITY&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; Long id;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;@Column&lt;/span&gt;(nullable &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; String status;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The standard APIs live in &lt;code&gt;jakarta.persistence&lt;/code&gt;. An implementation discovers a
&lt;code&gt;jakarta.persistence.spi.PersistenceProvider&lt;/code&gt; through Java&amp;rsquo;s service-provider
mechanism. The provider translates persistence operations and JPQL or Criteria
queries into database-specific SQL.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>